# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-eval-x-output-lost-on-cad — a failing -x form is reported DONE OK, silently
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: alfe-backend
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: feat-bricscad-print-check
#+DATE: 2026-09-25

* Observed

pjb, on the Windows machine, alfe 2.2.90, BricsCAD V25 under EPURE:

#+begin_example
alfe --keep-workdir -norc --verbose --debug --bricscad --epure \
  -x '(print (= "" (toutes_options nil)))' \
  -x '(print (equal (quote ((enabled) (message))) (f_DateHeure_UTC 0)))'
#+end_example

The run is a success by every measure alfe reports: READY after 17 s,
DONE 1 OK, DONE 2 OK, plan finished status=:SUCCESS exit=0, errors.txt
empty, protocol/stderr.txt empty. And it prints NOTHING:

#+begin_example
$ od -c protocol/stdout.txt
0000000  \r  \n  \r  \n
#+end_example

* Diagnosis — the error was swallowed, the output was never lost

The bracketing run pjb ran next settles it. The SAME session, with the
call bracketed by two princ:

#+begin_example
alfe --keep-workdir --debug --verbose -norc --bricscad --epure \
  -x '(princ 1)' -x '(print (toutes_options nil))' -x '(princ 2)'

protocol/stdout.txt:   1   2  \r  \n  \r  \n
#+end_example

=1= and =2= both arrive, adjacent, in one file: the output channel is
intact across the middle form, which contributed ZERO bytes -- not even
the leading newline =print= always writes. (The trailing =\r\n\r\n= is
the EPURE session's own trailer; it appears in every EPURE run,
including ones that print correctly, which is why pjb's first capture
consisted of exactly that pair and nothing else.)

A form that reaches =print= cannot emit nothing. The form never reached
it: =(toutes_options nil)= SIGNALLED, and alfe reported OK.

The bug is in alfe's own override of =autolisp-eval-request-form=,
emitted into run-common.lsp by
=autolisp-front-end/source/file-protocol.lisp:1377=:

#+begin_src lisp
(setq *AUTOLISP-EVAL-RESULT* nil)
(vl-catch-all-apply 'load (list path))   ; <- the outcome is DISCARDED
(setq r *AUTOLISP-EVAL-RESULT*)
#+end_src

The protocol server loop does the right thing --
=autolisp-protocol-server-loop= (autolisp-remote-io.lsp:265..291) calls
=autolisp-eval-request-form= under =vl-catch-all-apply= and, on an
error, logs =ERROR protocol request N: …= to protocol/stderr.txt, sets
rc 1 and =DONE N FAIL=. But the override catches the error FIRST, with
its own =vl-catch-all-apply=, and returns the untouched nil result. The
loop sees a clean return, so it writes =DONE N OK=.

So every =-x= form that signals -- undefined function, wrong arity, bad
argument type -- is reported as a success, with no diagnostic anywhere
and the form's output missing. That is the whole of this ticket; the
earlier readings (print broken, booleans, -x vs -l, EPURE) are all
refuted, and the four-way comparison that refuted them is kept below.

The =-l= path is unaffected: =autolisp-load-form-p= routes it to
=autolisp-eval-load-form=, which does not catch, so its errors reach
the loop and are reported.

* What it is NOT (the four-way comparison, verify:bricscad-print:windows)

The same three forms, none of which can signal, each way:

#+begin_example
1. loaded from a file       exit 0   \r\nT \r\nnil end
2. passed with -x           exit 0   \r\nT \r\nnil 42        (actions = 3)
3. under EPURE, from a file exit 0   \r\nT \r\nnil end\r\n\r\n
4. under EPURE, with -x     exit 0   \r\nT \r\nnil 42\r\n\r\n (actions = 3)
#+end_example

print works, booleans render, -x carries output, and an EPURE session
does not intercept the printer. All four print because none of those
forms fails -- which is exactly the blind spot.

* Why it matters

A user asking a CAD a question with =-x= gets exit 0 and silence when
his expression is wrong, with nothing to distinguish it from a working
run. It is worse than a lost diagnostic: it is a false SUCCESS, and a
script that tests alfe's exit code cannot see the failure at all. It
also cost this investigation several rounds -- the EPURE API validation
(alfe-plugin-epure-windows-validation.issue) read the silence three
times over as a fault in EPURE.

* Fix

Do not discard the load outcome. Keep the guard (so runtime flags are
still published), but re-signal afterwards so the server loop's own
handler does its documented job:

#+begin_src lisp
(setq *AUTOLISP-EVAL-RESULT* nil)
(setq err (vl-catch-all-apply 'load (list path)))
(if (vl-catch-all-error-p err)
  (setq err (vl-catch-all-error-message err))
  (progn (setq err nil) (setq r *AUTOLISP-EVAL-RESULT*)))
…
(alfe-publish-runtime-flags)
(if err (error err) r)
#+end_src

Re-signalling by MESSAGE keeps the quit-signal path working: the loop
recognises a quit by =autolisp-quit-signal-p= on the message.

* Acceptance

- An =-x= form that signals gives a non-zero exit, a =DONE N FAIL=
  status and an =ERROR protocol request N: …= line on stderr -- the
  same as the equivalent form loaded with =-l=.
- A form that succeeds is unaffected: =DONE N OK=, exit 0.
- A regression test covers both, without a CAD.
- pjb's own case reports the real error instead of printing nothing.

* Resolved -- alfe 2.2.91

The emitted override binds the load's outcome, and re-signals it by
message after publishing the runtime flags
(autolisp-front-end/source/file-protocol.lisp). The protocol server
loop then does what it always could: `DONE N FAIL', rc 1, and
`ERROR protocol request N: <the engine's own message>' on
protocol/stderr.txt; alfe exits non-zero.

Tested WITHOUT a CAD, which the suite had never done: clautolisp is
an AutoLISP engine, so it can host the emitted run-common.lsp and
answer for the runtime's own behaviour. Two tests in
autolisp-front-end/tests/file-protocol-tests.lisp:

- protocol-emitted-eval-override-resignals-the-load-error -- pins the
  emitted text, including that the ONLY occurrence of the load call
  is the one bound to ERR, so the discarding shape cannot come back.
- protocol-signalling-form-is-reported-fail-not-ok -- spawns
  clautolisp-sbcl on the emitted file and drives the protocol:
  `(no_such_function 1)' must publish DONE 1 FAIL with the diagnostic
  on stderr, and `(print (= 1 1))' must still publish DONE 2 OK with
  `T' on stdout. Red before the fix ("a signalling form must publish
  DONE 1 FAIL, got \"DONE 1 OK\"", stderr empty); green after. It is
  skipped where clautolisp-sbcl is not on disk.

Documented in RELEASE_NOTES.org (alfe / Backends) and in the alfe
user manual (§ Action output semantics), which states the new
contract and warns that a script written against the old silence
must be re-run.

Noticed while fixing this, filed separately:
alfe-cad-error-reported-twice-on-stderr.issue.
